why 1 1/4 OVERLAP The alto drops to D, below where the tenor just was, while the tenor falls to B. Both move down together, so the order stays clear.
why 4 3/4 DOUBLING Alto (A–B) and tenor (C♯–B) meet on B by step from opposite sides.
why 5 1/4 RANGE The tune is at its peak on a high G, and the bass rises B–C♯–D to stay close under the harmony. It falls back by step at once.
why 5 1/2 RANGE The top of the bass's climb, D. It walks back down C♯–B–A over the next beats.
why 5 5/8 RANGE The bass is heading back down from D and is in its usual range by the next beat.
why 7 3/8 CROSSING The tenor passes down G–F♯–E under the bass's held G for an eighth; then the bass drops to C♯.
why 8 0 HARMONIC_RHYTHM The D chord arrives on the last eighth and carries over the bar line while the bass leaps an octave. The new bar decorates the same chord before moving on.
why 8 0 RANGE The bass leaps up an octave to a high D and walks down D–C–B–A: a short high stretch that keeps it close to the tenor.
why 8 0 LEAPS The tenor rises E–A–D through chord notes as the bass leaps an octave. Leaping through the notes of the chord keeps the line clear.
why 8 3/4 SIMILAR_MOTION All four voices step down together into a diminished chord leading to E minor, led by the bass's walk G–F♯–E–D♯–E.
why 9 0 LEADING_TONE The alto holds F♯ over the E minor chord as a suspension and decorates its resolution, F♯–D♯–E, reaching E an eighth later.
why 9 3/4 SPACING The new phrase starts with the tune an octave higher, on F♯, while the alto stays on D. The tune steps down at once and the gap narrows.
